From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 20 Sep 2026 21:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] GUIWindowManager: don't hold the graphics context across a
 window's init

CGUIWindowManager::ActivateWindow takes the graphics context lock and holds it
through ActivateWindow_Internal all the way to the GUI_MSG_WINDOW_INIT the new
window receives. CGUIMediaWindow answers that message by reading its first
directory with m_backgroundLoad deliberately false, so the listing runs
synchronously - on a network source, for as long as the server takes.

Everything that needs the graphics context waits behind it for that whole time,
including the nested render loop the busy dialog uses to keep the UI alive. So
the one place that most needs the UI to keep drawing is the one place that
cannot.

Drop the lock around the init message, exactly as the dialog branch forty lines
above already does for Open(). Use CSingleExit and not unique_lock::unlock():
the graphics context is a counting recursive lock, so unlock() releases only one
level and does nothing at all when the caller already holds it deeper in the
stack, while CSingleExit unwinds to zero and restores on scope exit.

The contract this asks of a window is the one HandleAction already asks of every
action handler, and ActivateWindow's own dialog branch already asks of Open():
the window and dialog containers may move while the message is being handled.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

diff --git a/xbmc/guilib/GUIWindowManager.cpp b/xbmc/guilib/GUIWindowManager.cpp
index 05622aa..839f6be 100644
--- a/xbmc/guilib/GUIWindowManager.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/guilib/GUIWindowManager.cpp
@@ -968,7 +968,16 @@ void CGUIWindowManager::ActivateWindow_Internal(int iWindowID, const std::vector
   // Send the init message
   CGUIMessage msg(GUI_MSG_WINDOW_INIT, 0, 0, currentWindow, iWindowID);
   msg.SetStringParams(params);
-  pNewWindow->OnMessage(msg);
+  {
+    // A media window reads its first directory from inside this message, and
+    // does it synchronously. Holding the graphics context across a network
+    // listing stops anything else drawing - including the busy dialog's own
+    // render loop. The dialog branch above drops the lock the same way.
+    // CSingleExit, not lock.unlock(): the context is a counting recursive lock
+    // and unlock() would be a no-op whenever we are already nested.
+    CSingleExit exitit(CServiceBroker::GetWinSystem()->GetGfxContext());
+    pNewWindow->OnMessage(msg);
+  }
 //  CServiceBroker::GetGUI()->GetInfoManager().GetInfoProviders().GetGUIControlsInfoProvider().SetPreviousWindow(WINDOW_INVALID);
 }
 
